you never know if you're right.

00:21:40.240 --> 00:21:44.240
But I think that I'll
end up sharing a story

00:21:44.240 --> 00:21:48.340
where I did choose to
communicate something

00:21:48.340 --> 00:21:50.600
and it went sideways.

00:21:50.600 --> 00:21:55.600
So, communication alone is not
the key, it has to be proper.

00:21:55.680 --> 00:22:00.150
I think, and I don't know all the criteria

00:22:00.150 --> 00:22:04.570
but I think if you try to have
as much humility as possible

00:22:04.570 --> 00:22:07.270
so it's not a confrontation but,

00:22:07.270 --> 00:22:09.880
like the way you said it, that
was like a humble approach.

00:22:09.880 --> 00:22:14.150
It's like, hey, I just wanna
share how this makes me feel.

00:22:14.150 --> 00:22:17.010
I actually don't know what to conclude,

00:22:17.010 --> 00:22:20.630
I really would like to
hear from you about this

00:22:20.630 --> 00:22:24.660
because be very careful
when jumping to conclusions

00:22:25.570 --> 00:22:28.600
preemptively, especially
if it makes you upset,

00:22:28.600 --> 00:22:30.613
aggressive, something like that.

00:22:32.930 --> 00:22:36.360
And even me talking like
that, I started to get,

00:22:36.360 --> 00:22:37.630
I start to wig out when it feels like

00:22:37.630 --> 00:22:38.720
we're just giving advice.

00:22:38.720 --> 00:22:40.650
It's like, I don't like
being those people.

00:22:40.650 --> 00:22:44.070
So I like to share more
about just what we would do.

00:22:44.070 --> 00:22:46.983
But I do think that in this instance,

00:22:48.690 --> 00:22:50.810
you never know what the
other person is going through

00:22:50.810 --> 00:22:54.460
or if they're just, they
may just be oblivious

00:22:54.460 --> 00:22:58.270
to what they're doing and the
impact that it has on you.

00:22:58.270 --> 00:22:59.470
Hey, I made dinner.

00:22:59.470 --> 00:23:00.501
I mean we made plans.

00:23:00.501 --> 00:23:03.310
I sacrifice doing other things

00:23:03.310 --> 00:23:06.610
to make sure that we had this time

00:23:06.610 --> 00:23:08.750
because that's what plans do.

00:23:08.750 --> 00:23:12.960
Plans mean you're not planning
to do everything else.

00:23:12.960 --> 00:23:16.230
And I would say that the
vast majority of the time,

00:23:16.230 --> 00:23:20.870
assuming that what is going on

00:23:20.870 --> 00:23:23.083
is this person is just
a little bit flighty,

00:23:24.670 --> 00:23:29.530
you communicating that
this is a concern of yours

00:23:29.530 --> 00:23:31.880
and pointing it out will,

00:23:31.880 --> 00:23:36.770
I don't know, I'm not especially
schedule-oriented person

00:23:36.770 --> 00:23:39.340
but I'm not very flighty either.

00:23:39.340 --> 00:23:41.090
Like, if I say I'm gonna be somewhere,

00:23:41.090 --> 00:23:44.930
I'm gonna be there but my assumption

00:23:44.930 --> 00:23:48.460
is that people who are
kind of like scattered

00:23:48.460 --> 00:23:50.540
is that all it takes,

00:23:50.540 --> 00:23:51.830
what you're constantly doing

00:23:51.830 --> 00:23:53.020
with somebody's got that personality

00:23:53.020 --> 00:23:57.700
is you're kinda competing for
they're sort of unofficial

00:23:57.700 --> 00:23:59.170
floating priorities.

00:23:59.170 --> 00:24:00.003
Yeah.

00:24:00.003 --> 00:24:03.640
And so, by simply expressing
concern about something,

00:24:03.640 --> 00:24:05.400
you move yourself to the top of,

00:24:05.400 --> 00:24:07.440
and so the next time you
say you've got plans,

00:24:07.440 --> 00:24:09.370
they will be like, okay, I
know that I have a tendency

00:24:09.370 --> 00:24:12.500
to kinda flake out so
I don't wanna do that

00:24:12.500 --> 00:24:17.310
because Casanora said
that this was an issue

00:24:17.310 --> 00:24:19.003
and so I'm going to honor that.

00:24:20.144 --> 00:24:22.300
I think that's probably
what's gonna happen.

00:24:22.300 --> 00:24:24.850
Now, if you can't make that,

00:24:24.850 --> 00:24:26.490
if you can't communicate in that way,

00:24:26.490 --> 00:24:29.900
if you're a person who just
avoids conflict at all costs,

00:24:29.900 --> 00:24:32.653
then I think that you gotta make plan Bs.

00:24:33.670 --> 00:24:36.957
I think you got to have a
back-up plan that you're okay,

00:24:36.957 --> 00:24:41.200
you're not emotionally drained
by having to go to plan B.

00:24:41.200 --> 00:24:44.190
So, you'll be like, all right,
I got plans with you-know-who

00:24:44.190 --> 00:24:48.170
which means it might fall
through but if it doesn't happen,

00:24:48.170 --> 00:24:51.587
I'm gonna eat a whole deep-dish pizza

00:24:51.587 --> 00:24:53.442
and watch "Ghostbusters".

00:24:53.442 --> 00:24:54.310
Okay, yeah.

00:24:54.310 --> 00:24:57.430
You gotta have something
that you can look forward to

00:24:57.430 --> 00:24:59.620
so that you don't get pissed at them.

00:24:59.620 --> 00:25:00.453
Yeah.

00:25:00.453 --> 00:25:01.570
But you can kinda reward yourself

00:25:01.570 --> 00:25:04.125
and almost kind of hope they cancel.

00:25:04.125 --> 00:25:06.090
Well, yeah, I definitely agree

00:25:06.090 --> 00:25:09.060
that you got to find a way
to not harbor resentment

00:25:09.060 --> 00:25:10.220
towards this person

00:25:12.210 --> 00:25:14.703
because it's not gonna change overnight.

00:25:15.620 --> 00:25:17.940
The type of person that does this

00:25:17.940 --> 00:25:19.440
is gonna keep struggling with it

00:25:19.440 --> 00:25:22.960
even if you having a great conversation.

00:25:22.960 --> 00:25:24.420
It may take two or three times

00:25:24.420 --> 00:25:27.227
but it's gonna happen again
so you have to find a way,

00:25:27.227 --> 00:25:28.393
and maybe that's,

00:25:29.320 --> 00:25:31.780
you invite them to things
where there's other people

00:25:31.780 --> 00:25:35.370
so the plans aren't
contingent on that person

00:25:35.370 --> 00:25:37.600
flaking or not flaking, you know?

00:25:37.600 --> 00:25:38.433
Yeah, yeah, that's good.

00:25:38.433 --> 00:25:39.266
So, it's like the thing can continue

00:25:39.266 --> 00:25:40.990
and hey, you're invited if you wanna come

00:25:40.990 --> 00:25:42.930
but I've changed the way

00:25:42.930 --> 00:25:44.710
I invite you to things.
That's a good idea.

00:25:44.710 --> 00:25:46.230
Yeah.

00:25:46.230 --> 00:25:48.350
Let's move to another one.

00:25:48.350 --> 00:25:49.610
I know that there was the one that,

00:25:49.610 --> 00:25:50.470
it's like three different people.

00:25:50.470 --> 00:25:52.970
Yeah, so, three different
people basically asked

00:25:52.970 --> 00:25:56.530
a very similar question so I
will quickly read all these.

00:25:56.530 --> 00:25:57.880
First ones from postcardhs.

00:25:58.987 --> 00:26:00.357
"How do you reconnect with someone

00:26:00.357 --> 00:26:01.787
"that you used to be best friends with?

00:26:01.787 --> 00:26:03.727
"I had a best friend but ever ever since

00:26:03.727 --> 00:26:06.940
"we went to different
schools, we stopped talking."

00:26:06.940 --> 00:26:09.447
Second question from Cameron.

00:26:09.447 --> 00:26:11.447
"If you haven't talked
to someone for a while

00:26:11.447 --> 00:26:13.867
"and nothing really ended your friendship

00:26:13.867 --> 00:26:15.727
"like a fight or moving away, et cetera,

00:26:15.727 --> 00:26:17.247
"are you still friends with that person?

00:26:17.247 --> 00:26:18.807
"Should you reach out
and have a conversation

00:26:18.807 --> 00:26:20.550
"or should you just let it go?"

00:26:20.550 --> 00:26:23.147
And lainey, "How do I
reconnect with a friend

00:26:23.147 --> 00:26:24.597
"that I haven't hung out with regularly

00:26:24.597 --> 00:26:25.937
"since elementary school.

00:26:25.937 --> 00:26:27.389
"I have close neighborhood friends

00:26:27.389 --> 00:26:29.067
"that stopped hanging out with me

00:26:29.067 --> 00:26:31.097
"when our mutual friend died.

00:26:31.097 --> 00:26:33.657
"Should I move on or try
to befriend them again?

00:26:33.657 --> 00:26:36.460
"And if so, how?"

00:26:36.460 --> 00:26:37.730
Hmm, it's interesting

00:26:37.730 --> 00:26:41.340
because we've gone through life together

00:26:41.340 --> 00:26:43.720
so we've always had each other

00:26:43.720 --> 00:26:48.150
but we've also made really
good friends over the years

00:26:48.150 --> 00:26:50.693
that I do,

00:26:51.650 --> 00:26:54.150
I wonder if you would call it a pattern

00:26:54.150 --> 00:26:58.893
of like losing connection, you know?

00:27:00.320 --> 00:27:02.510
Everybody that we've connected with

00:27:02.510 --> 00:27:05.580
and it's like we got the grade school

00:27:05.580 --> 00:27:07.223
through high school friends,

00:27:08.110 --> 00:27:10.040
had some tight friendships there,

00:27:10.040 --> 00:27:14.450
then we had some really
tight college friends

00:27:14.450 --> 00:27:18.550
and then we had, there's like
a few post-college people

00:27:18.550 --> 00:27:21.670
and like I'm thinking of
specific people all along the way

00:27:21.670 --> 00:27:25.313
that when we, especially
when we moved out here,

00:27:28.380 --> 00:27:29.657
our friendship got more,

00:27:29.657 --> 00:27:32.260
well, we made friends out here, you know?

00:27:32.260 --> 00:27:36.423
But all those friends were
physically left behind but then,

00:27:39.610 --> 00:27:41.080
I think it's a continuing pattern

00:27:41.080 --> 00:27:44.390
of like not really
maintaining those friendships.

00:27:44.390 --> 00:27:46.830
And we discussed on a previous podcast

00:27:46.830 --> 00:27:51.830
about our issues with texting
and talking on the phone

00:27:52.330 --> 00:27:54.430
and neither of us are
good at either of those

00:27:54.430 --> 00:27:56.750
and we don't use texting
or talking on the phone

00:27:56.750 --> 00:27:58.270
really as a way to connect with people.

00:27:58.270 --> 00:27:59.430
It's usually a way to coordinate

00:27:59.430 --> 00:28:01.910
some particular strategic action

00:28:01.910 --> 00:28:03.900
and then you couple that with the fact

00:28:03.900 --> 00:28:06.300
that everybody's busy, I understand

00:28:06.300 --> 00:28:08.740
but our time is very much scheduled,

00:28:08.740 --> 00:28:10.400
like we've got friends,
there's a lot of people

00:28:10.400 --> 00:28:12.350
that we're friends with in Los Angeles

00:28:12.350 --> 00:28:15.850
and you'll be on a text thread with them

00:28:15.850 --> 00:28:20.040
and it'll be like 2:30 p.m. on a Wednesday

00:28:20.040 --> 00:28:22.400
and all of a sudden, they
start texting each other

00:28:22.400 --> 00:28:23.610
and they text each other

00:28:23.610 --> 00:28:25.370
and they're like putting memes in there

00:28:25.370 --> 00:28:27.860
and somebody's telling a story

00:28:27.860 --> 00:28:29.500
and somebody's responding to it.

00:28:29.500 --> 00:28:30.480
Right.

00:28:30.480 --> 00:28:35.480
2:30 p.m. on a Wednesday,
95 times out of 100,

00:28:35.690 --> 00:28:38.240
we're doing something that we just can't,

00:28:38.240 --> 00:28:40.380
I can't just be like, I'm
gonna get out of this meeting

00:28:40.380 --> 00:28:42.590
or this brainstorming
session or whatever it is

00:28:42.590 --> 00:28:44.380
and enter into this text chat.

00:28:44.380 --> 00:28:45.213
So, we don't--

00:28:45.213 --> 00:28:47.200
For all intents and purposes,

00:28:47.200 --> 00:28:49.250
before our working hours--

00:28:49.250 --> 00:28:50.160
We're unavailable.

00:28:50.160 --> 00:28:54.220
It's like we don't have
a phone for personal use.

00:28:54.220 --> 00:28:55.770
And then when we get
home, it's like, okay,

00:28:55.770 --> 00:28:57.940
I haven't seen my kids
and my wife all day,

00:28:57.940 --> 00:29:01.170
I'm gonna spend some
quality time with them,

00:29:01.170 --> 00:29:03.420
maybe I'll be able to stay up long enough

00:29:03.420 --> 00:29:05.810
to watch a television show
with my wife if I can do it

00:29:05.810 --> 00:29:08.000
but I'm gonna bed pretty
early so I can get up

00:29:08.000 --> 00:29:09.200
and go to the gym.

00:29:09.200 --> 00:29:12.380
It's like we don't leave a lot of space

00:29:12.380 --> 00:29:14.010
for connecting with people

00:29:14.010 --> 00:29:16.690
who are not close to us physically.

00:29:16.690 --> 00:29:19.020
It's gotta be like okay, all right,

00:29:19.020 --> 00:29:22.100
we're going out with this
couple on a double date

00:29:22.100 --> 00:29:24.800
on Friday night or we're
having this event at my house.

00:29:24.800 --> 00:29:26.210
Six weeks from now.

00:29:26.210 --> 00:29:28.290
It's like a lot of it has to be booked

00:29:28.290 --> 00:29:31.145
because then you got a couple
of nights on the weekend,

00:29:31.145 --> 00:29:35.120
you got the stuff that
your kids have planned

00:29:35.120 --> 00:29:37.350
that you got to attend and you got like,

00:29:37.350 --> 00:29:41.600
okay, you got relatives coming
into town, like your nieces.

00:29:41.600 --> 00:29:44.240
And so that weekend's off--
So, we have a difficult time

00:29:44.240 --> 00:29:46.540
just keeping up with the
people in Los Angeles

00:29:46.540 --> 00:29:48.180
and then you've got people who live

00:29:48.180 --> 00:29:49.450
in different parts of Los Angeles

00:29:49.450 --> 00:29:50.360
and if they're on the West side,

00:29:50.360 --> 00:29:52.815
they might as well be in South Carolina.

00:29:52.815 --> 00:29:55.470
And so then, everybody back
home in North Carolina,

00:29:55.470 --> 00:29:57.740
people that you've been
friends with for a long time,

00:29:57.740 --> 00:30:02.500
unless you're someone who can
text and talk on the phone

00:30:02.500 --> 00:30:04.460
for extended period of time,

00:30:04.460 --> 00:30:06.250
those friendships become,

00:30:06.250 --> 00:30:07.810
like we've got a number of friendships

00:30:07.810 --> 00:30:11.230
where I consider myself
very close to someone

00:30:11.230 --> 00:30:14.130
that is in North Carolina and if we decide

00:30:14.130 --> 00:30:17.170
to get together while
I'm in North Carolina

00:30:17.170 --> 00:30:19.460
or they come out here,
we have a great time

00:30:19.460 --> 00:30:21.730
and we pick up exactly where we left off

00:30:21.730 --> 00:30:24.990
and I think we've all kind of accepted

00:30:24.990 --> 00:30:26.500
this isn't the kind of friendship

00:30:26.500 --> 00:30:28.370
that we're gonna be like
relying on each other

00:30:28.370 --> 00:30:29.254
on a regular basis.

00:30:29.254 --> 00:30:32.070
This is something we re-enter into.

00:30:32.070 --> 00:30:33.800
Right, like two examples.

00:30:33.800 --> 00:30:37.890
So Mike from from back in North Carolina,

00:30:37.890 --> 00:30:40.340
work will bring him out here to California

00:30:40.340 --> 00:30:41.377
and sometimes, it may not be too close

00:30:41.377 --> 00:30:44.370
and maybe like Bakersfield
but then he'll make a point

00:30:44.370 --> 00:30:47.250
and we're like with a
lot of advance notice

00:30:47.250 --> 00:30:49.637
to like initiate with us and say,

00:30:49.637 --> 00:30:53.730
"Hey, I'm coming into town,
can we get together one night?"

00:30:53.730 --> 00:30:57.000
or "Let's go camping for a few nights."

00:30:57.000 --> 00:30:58.740
We used to do that, like once a year,

00:30:58.740 --> 00:30:59.630
we would go camping.

00:30:59.630 --> 00:31:02.710
It was something that like,
he would always initiate it

00:31:02.710 --> 00:31:04.250
because he was the one coming into town,

00:31:04.250 --> 00:31:06.020
he would give his heads up and we would,

00:31:06.020 --> 00:31:08.960
so we always make some time.

00:31:08.960 --> 00:31:10.310
Lately, over the past couple years,

00:31:10.310 --> 00:31:11.950
we haven't been able to make the time

00:31:11.950 --> 00:31:14.010
for the camping like we used to do

00:31:16.200 --> 00:31:18.540
but it's on his initiative.

00:31:18.540 --> 00:31:20.650
Whenever I go back to North Carolina,

00:31:20.650 --> 00:31:21.800
the time in is so limited,

00:31:21.800 --> 00:31:24.220
I'm just prioritizing my family

00:31:24.220 --> 00:31:28.740
and if I get to see one
friend when I'm there,

00:31:28.740 --> 00:31:31.850
I'm very lucky 'cause it's
like the once a year trip.

00:31:31.850 --> 00:31:34.200
And then you've got the other,

00:31:34.200 --> 00:31:39.030
another example like
Eric who lives in Utah.

00:31:39.030 --> 00:31:40.710
And so, when we go to,

00:31:40.710 --> 00:31:43.870
he lives in Park City so
when we would go to Sundance,

00:31:43.870 --> 00:31:46.210
well, I mean, if he found
out we were going there,

00:31:46.210 --> 00:31:48.650
we didn't see him, that
would be an insult.

00:31:48.650 --> 00:31:50.820
I mean, it's like, because,

00:31:50.820 --> 00:31:51.870
I mean he's a high school friend,

00:31:51.870 --> 00:31:55.008
he was in the Wax Paper Dogz with us.

00:31:55.008 --> 00:31:58.400
We don't really communicate at all

00:31:58.400 --> 00:32:01.170
except when we happen to see
him there or like when we went,

00:32:01.170 --> 00:32:02.710
we went to something else.

00:32:02.710 --> 00:32:03.860
Oh, when we had a show.

00:32:03.860 --> 00:32:06.220
We introduced him to the
audience in Utah in Salt Lake.

00:32:06.220 --> 00:32:08.112
Yeah, in Salt Lake City.

00:32:08.112 --> 00:32:13.112
And so, but when we see
him or when we see Mike,

00:32:13.370 --> 00:32:16.690
it's like the friendship picks
up right where it left off

00:32:16.690 --> 00:32:21.530
and I think those are two
examples of like an understanding.

00:32:21.530 --> 00:32:24.300
I think that in a lot of ways,

00:32:24.300 --> 00:32:27.623
those individuals like Mike or Eric have,

00:32:28.900 --> 00:32:31.280
I feel like they've kind of
adjusted to us in some way

00:32:31.280 --> 00:32:33.870
but that's my assumption.

00:32:33.870 --> 00:32:36.030
I know Mike, because I've
been friends with him

00:32:36.030 --> 00:32:37.510
for a long time, is the kind of guy

00:32:37.510 --> 00:32:39.960
who will get on the phone
and talk for a long time.

00:32:39.960 --> 00:32:44.120
Like, his job requires him
to drive a lot of places

00:32:44.120 --> 00:32:47.478
and he'll talk to people
on the phone and he can--

00:32:47.478 --> 00:32:48.990
But he doesn't talk to you, right?

00:32:48.990 --> 00:32:49.823
You don't talk to him on the phone.

00:32:49.823 --> 00:32:51.160
No, very rarely.

00:32:51.160 --> 00:32:52.895
It's like we'll text and
we'll get something set up

00:32:52.895 --> 00:32:56.100
and then, of course, then we
have like long conversations,

00:32:56.100 --> 00:32:57.910
the three of us once we're doing something

00:32:57.910 --> 00:32:59.720
like camping together or whatever.

00:32:59.720 --> 00:33:02.330
Right, and like we got the friend who,

00:33:02.330 --> 00:33:06.400
he went back to Australia for like weeks.

00:33:06.400 --> 00:33:07.233
Yeah.

00:33:07.233 --> 00:33:09.620
And it's like, we're
really tight but it's,

00:33:09.620 --> 00:33:13.160
like I find the conversations
that we have over text,

00:33:13.160 --> 00:33:15.240
it's more like when you coming back.

00:33:15.240 --> 00:33:16.073
Mm-hmm.

00:33:16.073 --> 00:33:17.740
It's not like let's
continue our friendship

00:33:17.740 --> 00:33:21.710
while you're gone because I'm just

00:33:21.710 --> 00:33:26.610
so bad continuing a friendship over text.

00:33:26.610 --> 00:33:29.390
So I find that it's like,
it's all just very practical.

00:33:29.390 --> 00:33:31.230
And I think we've talked about that before

00:33:31.230 --> 00:33:33.920
and like learning how
there is an opportunity

00:33:33.920 --> 00:33:37.770
for text to be a legitimate way
to continue a friendship but

00:33:39.699 --> 00:33:43.990
I have this sense of guilt and obligation

00:33:43.990 --> 00:33:48.010
whenever like I fall out
of connection with somebody

00:33:48.010 --> 00:33:52.760
and I think I just put that
on myself and over the years,

00:33:52.760 --> 00:33:54.850
I feel like when the
two of us talk about it,

00:33:54.850 --> 00:33:57.313
we've said things that
have made me feel better

00:33:57.313 --> 00:33:59.900
that I don't know if
they're true or healthy

00:33:59.900 --> 00:34:01.430
but we say things like
well, you know what?

00:34:01.430 --> 00:34:03.670
I'm not good at text, I'm not
good at long-term friendships

00:34:03.670 --> 00:34:06.750
but when I see him, it would be fine.

00:34:06.750 --> 00:34:08.920
But like a great example is Greg.

00:34:08.920 --> 00:34:10.800
We were super tight with Greg in college

00:34:10.800 --> 00:34:15.800
and like he's working
up in Washington State

00:34:16.140 --> 00:34:18.870
and doing his own thing
and like we saw him

00:34:18.870 --> 00:34:20.770
we went on tour up
there, like they came in

00:34:20.770 --> 00:34:23.590
but it's not like,
occasionally, he'll text me

00:34:23.590 --> 00:34:25.920
and then we'll have an exchange that'll,

00:34:27.160 --> 00:34:32.130
it'll fizzle out over
the course of 10 minutes.

00:34:32.130 --> 00:34:35.600
And then in the past, there
would it be like let's have,

00:34:35.600 --> 00:34:37.540
we would hop on the
phone a couple of times.

00:34:37.540 --> 00:34:39.913
We'd have like an
hour-long catch-up session.

00:34:40.920 --> 00:34:44.180
But like, I mean I love my mama

00:34:44.180 --> 00:34:48.100
but I have a really hard
time calling her on the phone

00:34:48.100 --> 00:34:49.981
and reconnecting.

00:34:49.981 --> 00:34:51.500
But I have this sense of guilt

00:34:51.500 --> 00:34:54.400
that's like I don't talk to
my mom enough or like I don't,

00:34:55.280 --> 00:35:00.280
like, I wish that I had a
more of an active friendship

00:35:01.240 --> 00:35:06.010
with Greg because he's so busy
with his work, much like us,

00:35:06.010 --> 00:35:07.580
it's like he doesn't have the opportunity

00:35:07.580 --> 00:35:08.760
to come down here and visit

00:35:08.760 --> 00:35:10.820
and we don't have the opportunity
to go up there and visit

00:35:10.820 --> 00:35:12.573
and so it's,

00:35:13.431 --> 00:35:17.853
that's been difficult because I actually,

00:35:18.810 --> 00:35:21.330
I've moved from feeling, I don't know,

00:35:21.330 --> 00:35:23.993
I moved from feeling like
this is just the way it is.

00:35:25.950 --> 00:35:28.980
I still love the guy
and I know he loves me

00:35:28.980 --> 00:35:30.339
but the circumstances make it

00:35:30.339 --> 00:35:32.230
whoa, our friendship can't be active

00:35:33.410 --> 00:35:35.830
to feeling bad about it

00:35:36.980 --> 00:35:39.179
and it kind of
back-and-forth, I don't know.

00:35:39.179 --> 00:35:40.693
Yeah, I just,

00:35:42.410 --> 00:35:47.267
I don't like, I don't want
to put that on myself.

00:35:49.560 --> 00:35:50.750
You know what I'm saying?

00:35:50.750 --> 00:35:51.603
Like I don't,

00:35:53.560 --> 00:35:55.310
I know I can make all kinds of excuses

00:35:55.310 --> 00:35:59.433
but I just don't want to,

00:36:01.140 --> 00:36:02.610
I don't think it's
healthy for me personally

00:36:02.610 --> 00:36:06.960
to sit around and feel guilty about it.

00:36:06.960 --> 00:36:08.093
Yeah, yeah.

00:36:09.330 --> 00:36:13.090
Now, it's one thing if the person

00:36:13.090 --> 00:36:16.070
is going through something
specifically challenging

00:36:16.070 --> 00:36:18.140
and they need me.

00:36:18.140 --> 00:36:18.973
Yeah.

00:36:20.723 --> 00:36:23.423
None of the people that I'm
thinking about right now

00:36:24.730 --> 00:36:27.090
don't have some people in their lives

00:36:27.090 --> 00:36:30.130
that can meet some of those needs.

00:36:30.130 --> 00:36:32.750
I'm not enough I'm not
in a place to be like,

00:36:32.750 --> 00:36:35.410
what can I do for you tangibly?

00:36:35.410 --> 00:36:36.243
Mm-hmm.

00:36:37.110 --> 00:36:42.110
And so, I have just kind
of become okay with.

00:36:42.590 --> 00:36:43.790
The nature of these friendships

00:36:43.790 --> 00:36:48.230
is that they pick up where
they left off when they do

00:36:49.130 --> 00:36:52.180
and that's a great thing and
there's no hard feelings.

00:36:52.180 --> 00:36:55.503
And I just feel like trying
to make anything more of it.

00:36:59.070 --> 00:37:02.397
I don't know, I just feel
like I'm setting myself up

00:37:02.397 --> 00:37:04.290
for disappointment,

00:37:04.290 --> 00:37:06.040
both disappointment of myself

00:37:06.040 --> 00:37:08.730
and them being disappointed in me

00:37:08.730 --> 00:37:11.910
if I like start trying to
say hey, actually the dynamic

00:37:11.910 --> 00:37:12.810
of this thing is gonna change,

00:37:12.810 --> 00:37:15.360
like we're going to have this dynamic

00:37:15.360 --> 00:37:19.600
long-distance friendship
via text or via phone call

00:37:19.600 --> 00:37:21.470
every now and again.

00:37:21.470 --> 00:37:23.850
I just know that it's not
gonna be a reliable thing

00:37:23.850 --> 00:37:24.707
and I just don't wanna

00:37:24.707 --> 00:37:26.707
begin it.
It's not realistic for you.

00:37:27.911 --> 00:37:30.660
Yeah, I think it'll become more clear

00:37:30.660 --> 00:37:34.310
that I do have some neurosis
associated with this

00:37:34.310 --> 00:37:39.203
but I would like to believe,

00:37:40.320 --> 00:37:44.370
to build on your point,
that if we heard that Greg

00:37:44.370 --> 00:37:47.400
or someone in his family
was going through something,

00:37:47.400 --> 00:37:50.140
that if we knew we could
be of help by being there,

00:37:50.140 --> 00:37:51.960
that we would do everything we can

00:37:51.960 --> 00:37:54.863
to drop what we're doing
to go up there, you know?

00:37:55.882 --> 00:37:59.370
If there was something bad that happened,

00:37:59.370 --> 00:38:02.140
if they were going through something.

00:38:03.020 --> 00:38:04.370
But in the absence of that,

00:38:06.053 --> 00:38:08.210
an occasional text, it's like,

00:38:08.210 --> 00:38:10.260
hey, you crossed my mind, you know?

00:38:12.180 --> 00:38:16.890
I still aspire to like, to
at least dabble in that.

00:38:16.890 --> 00:38:17.723
Yeah.

00:38:17.723 --> 00:38:22.310
And it's interesting because
we were closest with Greg

00:38:22.310 --> 00:38:25.940
and then, in college,
and then Tim moved in

00:38:25.940 --> 00:38:28.160
and then like Tim was
really close with Matt

00:38:28.160 --> 00:38:29.280
and we got close to Matt

00:38:29.280 --> 00:38:32.040
and then, you know, we got
a text from Matt yesterday

00:38:32.040 --> 00:38:33.680
and all he was doing was asking

00:38:33.680 --> 00:38:36.600
if we had listened to an album, you know?

00:38:36.600 --> 00:38:38.020
Did you get that text because--

00:38:38.020 --> 00:38:40.370
I did and I listened to the album

00:38:40.370 --> 00:38:42.880
but you responded on the text thread

00:38:42.880 --> 00:38:43.830
and then I never got around

00:38:43.830 --> 00:38:45.180
to responding on the text thread,

00:38:45.180 --> 00:38:47.720
which is an interesting
thing that happens sometimes

00:38:47.720 --> 00:38:50.450
where if somebody is sending
something to the both of us,

00:38:50.450 --> 00:38:52.530
whether it's an email or a text,

00:38:52.530 --> 00:38:55.470
I've got this thing in my mind
that if one of us responds,

00:38:55.470 --> 00:38:56.910
the other one isn't obligated to

00:38:56.910 --> 00:38:58.260
and I know that that's not what this was,

00:38:58.260 --> 00:38:59.360
this wasn't a business thing.

00:38:59.360 --> 00:39:01.160
This was like hey have you
listened to this album?

00:39:01.160 --> 00:39:01.993
Yeah.

00:39:01.993 --> 00:39:03.620
And so I was actually thinking
about that a second ago.

00:39:03.620 --> 00:39:05.854
I was like, yeah, I haven't
gotten back on that text thread

00:39:05.854 --> 00:39:08.860
to be like, I also don't like the album

00:39:08.860 --> 00:39:09.910
that you recommended.

00:39:11.960 --> 00:39:13.450
But I only listened to it one time.

00:39:13.450 --> 00:39:14.487
And so then he was like,

00:39:14.487 --> 00:39:15.950
"Oh, you gotta listen to
it two or three times."

00:39:15.950 --> 00:39:17.140
So then I was like, okay, I'm gonna listen

00:39:17.140 --> 00:39:18.810
to this album again and
then I'm gonna get in

00:39:18.810 --> 00:39:19.690
on the text thread

00:39:19.690 --> 00:39:23.460
but I could also just say
that on the text thread.

00:39:23.460 --> 00:39:25.010
But I think it was cool that,

00:39:27.242 --> 00:39:31.720
and he, like, either every single time

00:39:31.720 --> 00:39:32.790
or the vast majority of time,

00:39:32.790 --> 00:39:37.080
he initiates the
conversation because we just,

00:39:37.080 --> 00:39:41.300
like I don't know if we've ever
initiated a text with Matt.

00:39:41.300 --> 00:39:44.350
But like every month or so,
there's like a little exchange

00:39:44.350 --> 00:39:47.170
and it's actually valuable

00:39:49.560 --> 00:39:51.640
for me to like, for what it is

00:39:51.640 --> 00:39:54.020
and for me not to attach
some sort of guilt

00:39:54.020 --> 00:39:55.010
that I'm not doing more.
It's also also valuable

00:39:55.010 --> 00:39:57.033
because he's introduced us,

00:39:58.690 --> 00:40:01.690
Matt is the one who
introduced us to Sturgill

00:40:03.170 --> 00:40:04.773
and then Jason Isbell.

00:40:05.620 --> 00:40:07.350
I don't think Sturgill
'cause I think we went

00:40:07.350 --> 00:40:10.410
in Amoeba Records and I
literally saw the record

00:40:10.410 --> 00:40:11.530
and I was like who is this?

00:40:11.530 --> 00:40:12.720
Okay, well, I know for a fact

00:40:12.720 --> 00:40:14.710
he introduced me to Jason Isbell.

00:40:14.710 --> 00:40:17.830
And I am as thankful for that

00:40:17.830 --> 00:40:20.210
as any music recommendation
that I've ever gotten.

00:40:20.210 --> 00:40:22.540
Oh, wow, and then you've got Tim,

00:40:22.540 --> 00:40:26.290
who I think because of
result of some of the stories

00:40:26.290 --> 00:40:29.940
we told on this podcast
that he started to reach out

00:40:29.940 --> 00:40:31.868
to discuss those with us and--

00:40:31.868 --> 00:40:33.380
Correct our errors.

00:40:33.380 --> 00:40:35.520
Correct our errors but it led to like--

00:40:35.520 --> 00:40:36.353
Fact check.

00:40:36.353 --> 00:40:39.070
Us having more of a
conversation and connecting more

00:40:39.070 --> 00:40:40.087
and I really enjoyed that.

00:40:40.087 --> 00:40:42.630
It was like, it was a
little odd that's like,

00:40:42.630 --> 00:40:45.313
okay, he's listening to "Ear
Biscuits" and then he's,

00:40:46.500 --> 00:40:47.770
and I'll tell you why it was odd.

00:40:47.770 --> 00:40:48.920
I'll try to articulate that,

00:40:48.920 --> 00:40:50.970
and then because he's probably listening.

00:40:51.870 --> 00:40:55.823
But it felt odd to me
'cause it was just like,

00:40:56.670 --> 00:41:00.093
okay, he's sitting on a
conversation we're having

00:41:00.093 --> 00:41:03.300
but we're not having it
with him, so it's like,

00:41:03.300 --> 00:41:06.380
I don't know, I have all this
guilt associated with friends

00:41:06.380 --> 00:41:09.970
because I'm like, I feel bad
you're listening to our podcast

00:41:09.970 --> 00:41:11.240
but you can't be here at the table

00:41:11.240 --> 00:41:13.480
to be a part of the conversation.

00:41:13.480 --> 00:41:14.410
Like if we were,

00:41:14.410 --> 00:41:16.550
if we had an active friendship
like we used to have,

00:41:16.550 --> 00:41:19.000
like, you'd have a seat at the table.

00:41:19.000 --> 00:41:22.540
And I know that's not
healthy and I don't know,

00:41:22.540 --> 00:41:25.313
he's not thinking that
but I start to think like,

00:41:26.750 --> 00:41:31.150
if Greg or Tim or Matt
listen to our podcast,

00:41:31.150 --> 00:41:32.610
did they start to feel like

00:41:34.300 --> 00:41:36.570
we're not good friends
or something to them

00:41:36.570 --> 00:41:39.740
because we never initiate
conversations with them.

00:41:39.740 --> 00:41:41.960
I don't know why I attach so much

00:41:45.610 --> 00:41:47.160
guilt to these things but I do.

00:41:48.510 --> 00:41:50.810
Actually, I guess I'll share this story

00:41:53.821 --> 00:41:56.110
where it really came to a head.

00:41:56.110 --> 00:41:57.367
A friend out here in LA,

00:41:57.367 --> 00:41:59.930
and I'm gonna try to be very
careful when telling this story

00:41:59.930 --> 00:42:01.600
because this story's about me.

00:42:01.600 --> 00:42:04.293
It's not about him at all.

00:42:06.370 --> 00:42:08.890
But it's about a text exchange that I had

00:42:10.600 --> 00:42:11.597
because I was,

00:42:13.380 --> 00:42:17.280
he's a friend that, like, we
were close a couple years ago

00:42:17.280 --> 00:42:18.607
like right when we move to LA but then,

00:42:18.607 --> 00:42:21.650
it was like, because purely,

00:42:21.650 --> 00:42:23.550
because of circumstantial reasons,

00:42:23.550 --> 00:42:27.280
we fell out of being in contact.

00:42:27.280 --> 00:42:29.660
Like it went from seeing
each other every week

00:42:29.660 --> 00:42:30.880
or every few weeks.

00:42:30.880 --> 00:42:34.050
Even if you didn't plan
it, we would see each other

00:42:34.050 --> 00:42:35.830
and having little conversations

00:42:35.830 --> 00:42:38.540
then kind of trickles
into, hey, let's make plans

00:42:38.540 --> 00:42:40.200
or we're going here for
lunch, you wanna come

00:42:40.200 --> 00:42:42.080
type of thing too like none of that.

00:42:42.080 --> 00:42:44.150
We're just in different circles.

00:42:44.150 --> 00:42:47.060
Kind of like what some people
ask, how do you reconnect?

00:42:47.060 --> 00:42:50.690
And so then, it would be like
once, maybe twice a year,

00:42:50.690 --> 00:42:54.760
we'd see each other or he would come over

00:42:54.760 --> 00:42:56.970
and we would have this
reconnection session

00:42:56.970 --> 00:43:00.030
that would be like an
hour or maybe two hours.

00:43:00.030 --> 00:43:02.160
And it would be great to reconnect

00:43:02.160 --> 00:43:05.050
but then it would it would
be like radio silence

00:43:05.050 --> 00:43:06.793
from both parties for a long time.

00:43:07.770 --> 00:43:10.713
And I guess one day,

00:43:12.100 --> 00:43:14.090
it caught me at a bad time

00:43:14.090 --> 00:43:17.120
that he decided to text me out of the blue

00:43:17.120 --> 00:43:19.320
and it was just a friendly text.

00:43:19.320 --> 00:43:24.320
It was something like, miss
you, man or thinking about you.

00:43:27.150 --> 00:43:27.983
And

00:43:30.916 --> 00:43:34.120
I've been struggling with, in general,

00:43:34.120 --> 00:43:35.330
and you know where the story's going

00:43:35.330 --> 00:43:38.980
but I was struggling
with if I feel something

00:43:38.980 --> 00:43:42.520
or if I think something, I should say it.

00:43:42.520 --> 00:43:45.590
So, just in general, like
I'm trying to become a person

00:43:45.590 --> 00:43:50.590
who's more honest about what I'm thinking

00:43:51.630 --> 00:43:53.510
or feeling or going through

00:43:53.510 --> 00:43:57.630
or how I feel like I
should respond to somebody

00:43:57.630 --> 00:44:00.000
and I thought it would
be a healthy practice

00:44:00.870 --> 00:44:05.870
for me to respond to his texts
with exactly what I thought,

00:44:06.830 --> 00:44:09.000
what I thought in the moment.

00:44:09.000 --> 00:44:10.963
And but what I was thinking was,

00:44:14.860 --> 00:44:16.670
well, you know what?

00:44:16.670 --> 00:44:18.580
I'll read what I wrote back.

00:44:18.580 --> 00:44:20.600
Okay, I remember this.

00:44:20.600 --> 00:44:22.200
He's like, "Dude, I miss you."

00:44:23.170 --> 00:44:24.303
That was his text.

00:44:25.230 --> 00:44:27.600
Again, this is about me, not him.

00:44:27.600 --> 00:44:29.223
That was all his text said.

00:44:30.970 --> 00:44:34.113
And I was like, I'm just gonna,

00:44:35.020 --> 00:44:39.670
I got a lot of feelings are
coming up in me and I just,

00:44:42.060 --> 00:44:43.957
and this is what I wrote.

00:44:43.957 --> 00:44:46.837
"Hey, man, thanks for the message.

00:44:46.837 --> 00:44:50.880
"Honestly, it's very tough
for me to know how to respond

00:44:51.747 --> 00:44:54.457
"so I'll just be real about it.

00:44:54.457 --> 00:44:58.607
"I care about you but I just
don't have time in my life

00:44:58.607 --> 00:45:01.857
"where I'm at right now
with family and work

00:45:01.857 --> 00:45:06.017
"to devote enough time to take
the friendship we had before

00:45:06.017 --> 00:45:07.623
"and make it work now.

00:45:08.817 --> 00:45:11.227
"There are a few friends in my life now

00:45:11.227 --> 00:45:13.227
"that I'm actively trying to maintain

00:45:13.227 --> 00:45:15.107
"a quality friendship with

00:45:15.107 --> 00:45:17.567
"and it is proving to be very difficult

00:45:17.567 --> 00:45:20.417
"purely from a practical standpoint.

00:45:20.417 --> 00:45:23.317
"I wish it weren't the
case but I respect you

00:45:23.317 --> 00:45:26.637
"and wanted to be honest
about it so I'm writing this

00:45:26.637 --> 00:45:28.617
"from a place of love.

00:45:28.617 --> 00:45:30.397
"Thanks for hearing me out, man."

00:45:32.490 --> 00:45:34.600
And you're laughing because

00:45:36.320 --> 00:45:39.140
why on earth would anybody

00:45:39.140 --> 00:45:43.620
who has an ounce of
kindness in their heart

00:45:43.620 --> 00:45:47.250
write this in response to a friend

00:45:47.250 --> 00:45:49.547
who simply wrote "Dude, I miss you."

00:45:51.370 --> 00:45:52.970
Right, is that what you're laughing at?

00:45:52.970 --> 00:45:54.920
Well, no, I'm laughing

00:45:54.920 --> 00:45:58.979
because it's just such a Link thing.

00:45:58.979 --> 00:46:02.140
It's just, it's comical to me.

00:46:02.140 --> 00:46:04.110
It was an experiment in honesty

00:46:04.110 --> 00:46:06.110
because well I feel guilty about,

00:46:06.110 --> 00:46:07.810
everything that I felt is true.

00:46:07.810 --> 00:46:10.480
As your best friend, I
wanna comment on that

00:46:10.480 --> 00:46:13.030
because I find that very
interesting that that,

00:46:13.030 --> 00:46:14.840
first of all, I think your
instincts are great, right?

00:46:14.840 --> 00:46:19.630
I think that the guilt that
you feel about friendships

00:46:19.630 --> 00:46:22.140
and your sense of obligation

00:46:22.140 --> 00:46:24.680
and your obligation to
be honest with people

00:46:24.680 --> 00:46:26.340
and these are all really good things.

00:46:26.340 --> 00:46:30.073
This shows that you're
a person of character.

00:46:32.020 --> 00:46:35.460
I think that it's interesting

00:46:35.460 --> 00:46:38.310
that you made a conscious
decision to be like,

00:46:38.310 --> 00:46:41.490
I feel like I need to
say what I think more

00:46:42.450 --> 00:46:47.380
because on the spectrum of
people who say what they think,

00:46:47.380 --> 00:46:49.030
you're pretty high on the spectrum

00:46:49.030 --> 00:46:52.140
of the frequency with which
you say what you think--

00:46:52.140 --> 00:46:53.430
Candor.

00:46:53.430 --> 00:46:57.390
Is pretty high, potentially
the highest of anyone

00:46:57.390 --> 00:46:58.630
that I know personally.

00:46:58.630 --> 00:47:00.190
Okay, okay.

00:47:00.190 --> 00:47:01.990
If you can name a person

00:47:01.990 --> 00:47:06.170
who says what they think about
things more often than you

00:47:06.170 --> 00:47:07.803
that we know collectively,

00:47:08.720 --> 00:47:10.970
I would be interested to
know who that person is.

00:47:10.970 --> 00:47:13.810
I can think of one person that we knew

00:47:14.710 --> 00:47:17.383
and I didn't much like this
person for that reason.

00:47:18.700 --> 00:47:19.533
So.

00:47:19.533 --> 00:47:22.500
That person would always say
exactly what they thought.

00:47:22.500 --> 00:47:24.650
And so, and of course--

00:47:24.650 --> 00:47:28.300
With no concern for how
it would be received.

00:47:28.300 --> 00:47:31.585
And so, yeah, and my tendency,

00:47:31.585 --> 00:47:33.280
not that I don't talk a lot,

00:47:33.280 --> 00:47:34.810
not that I don't have a lot of opinions

00:47:34.810 --> 00:47:38.060
but in certain situations like this,

00:47:38.060 --> 00:47:41.000
it's just like I'm always gonna be on team

00:47:41.940 --> 00:47:43.990
just say something to be nice

00:47:45.700 --> 00:47:49.130
to just keep things where they're at.

00:47:49.130 --> 00:47:51.690
Don't push away and don't pull that,

00:47:51.690 --> 00:47:52.753
like my tendencies to say something

00:47:52.753 --> 00:47:57.340
that just kind of keeps
things at a stasis.

00:47:57.340 --> 00:47:59.380
That's just my instinct, I'm
not saying it's good or bad

00:47:59.380 --> 00:48:02.203
but in every type of interaction,

00:48:02.203 --> 00:48:05.110
I don't wanna move things
further in either direction

00:48:05.110 --> 00:48:08.400
and that's just my sort
of knee-jerk response

00:48:08.400 --> 00:48:10.100
to any kind of interaction like that.

00:48:10.100 --> 00:48:14.840
Well, it just drove me crazy to feel like

00:48:14.840 --> 00:48:19.570
I respected this person,
this friend of mine

00:48:19.570 --> 00:48:23.270
that I couldn't give them what I felt like

00:48:23.270 --> 00:48:27.543
a true friend would give and so just,

00:48:28.530 --> 00:48:29.363
I felt like I needed

00:48:29.363 --> 00:48:32.303
to define the relationship, so to speak.

00:48:33.270 --> 00:48:34.103
Well, and I--

00:48:34.103 --> 00:48:34.936
Out of respect.

00:48:34.936 --> 00:48:36.700
But I will say in addition to this,

00:48:36.700 --> 00:48:38.440
to add more color to this situation

00:48:38.440 --> 00:48:40.060
without getting into specifics,

00:48:40.060 --> 00:48:43.090
that this person was going
through something difficult

00:48:43.090 --> 00:48:46.660
and had reached out to both of us

00:48:47.790 --> 00:48:49.930
and spent some time kind of catching us up

00:48:49.930 --> 00:48:51.720
on what they were going through

00:48:51.720 --> 00:48:53.950
and it was obvious,

00:48:53.950 --> 00:48:57.610
it was obvious that they wanted

00:48:57.610 --> 00:49:00.950
and needed friends to kinda

00:49:02.350 --> 00:49:04.880
help them get through this situation.

00:49:04.880 --> 00:49:06.800
And so there was a,

00:49:06.800 --> 00:49:08.360
we had similar interactions.

00:49:08.360 --> 00:49:11.150
You with him and then
me with him separately.

00:49:11.150 --> 00:49:12.980
I don't think we got
together the three of us.

00:49:12.980 --> 00:49:13.970
No.

00:49:13.970 --> 00:49:15.903
And I felt like, it was weird for me

00:49:15.903 --> 00:49:19.440
because I was like, I don't
think I can give this person

00:49:19.440 --> 00:49:22.230
what they need right now for a number

00:49:22.230 --> 00:49:24.860
of really complex reasons but I just,

00:49:24.860 --> 00:49:27.590
and so and maybe, you communicate,

00:49:27.590 --> 00:49:30.220
he didn't sent me a text
that said, "I miss you,"

00:49:30.220 --> 00:49:32.700
because I assume that
when you're interacting,

00:49:32.700 --> 00:49:35.240
you felt more of an obligation to be like,

00:49:35.240 --> 00:49:36.960
okay, I can see what this guy needs

00:49:36.960 --> 00:49:39.200
and I'm going to try to give it to him,

00:49:39.200 --> 00:49:42.340
which that's not good on
me, that's good on you.

00:49:42.340 --> 00:49:45.005
I mean I'm probably giving out the vibe

00:49:45.005 --> 00:49:47.330
that's just like, okay
I can see that Rhett

00:49:47.330 --> 00:49:49.840
is not available to me in this way.

00:49:49.840 --> 00:49:51.900
And so, but he may have thought

00:49:51.900 --> 00:49:53.950
that you were available to him in that way

00:49:53.950 --> 00:49:57.190
so you probably sensed
a little bit of pressure

00:49:57.190 --> 00:49:58.310
when he sends that text.

00:49:58.310 --> 00:50:01.913
It's like hey, I still need
you to help me through this.

00:50:01.913 --> 00:50:06.437
And his response was
very gracious, very kind

00:50:09.400 --> 00:50:12.013
but it was also clarifying that,

00:50:12.990 --> 00:50:15.130
I mean he was basically saying

00:50:15.130 --> 00:50:17.730
I was just being friendly,

00:50:17.730 --> 00:50:19.300
this wasn't coming from a place of me

00:50:19.300 --> 00:50:20.713
needing something from you.

00:50:22.670 --> 00:50:24.363
I just wanted,

00:50:25.300 --> 00:50:28.650
it was more of just like
a friendly, we're friends.

00:50:28.650 --> 00:50:29.650
Just checking in.

00:50:29.650 --> 00:50:32.737
Yeah, it was just like,

00:50:32.737 --> 00:50:35.143
and it wasn't anything more than that.

00:50:36.199 --> 00:50:37.760
And there was a little
bit of an exchange there

00:50:37.760 --> 00:50:40.120
where we clarified what each other meant

00:50:40.120 --> 00:50:45.120
and then a day later, I
wrote back one last thing

00:50:45.810 --> 00:50:49.827
and I said, "I read back through my text

00:50:49.827 --> 00:50:51.067
"and just wanted to apologize

00:50:51.067 --> 00:50:53.367
"for giving what must have
felt like a cold-hearted

00:50:53.367 --> 00:50:56.647
"out of the blue response
to a friendly kind text.

00:50:56.647 --> 00:50:58.577
"I was trying to respect
you by being honest

00:50:58.577 --> 00:51:00.427
"about what I was able to give

00:51:00.427 --> 00:51:03.087
"even though you were
not asking for anything

00:51:03.087 --> 00:51:05.727
"and I'm sorry if it insulted
or otherwise hurt you.

00:51:05.727 --> 00:51:07.307
"you deserve better and I think

00:51:07.307 --> 00:51:11.160
"that's what I was
actually trying to say."

00:51:11.160 --> 00:51:15.350
And he responded, "I appreciate that."

00:51:15.350 --> 00:51:20.350
And that, I mean, because
of my experiment in honesty,

00:51:21.930 --> 00:51:24.210
I'll call it, air quotes,

00:51:24.210 --> 00:51:27.900
that was apparently the
end of our friendship.

00:51:27.900 --> 00:51:30.049
It was definitely the
end of that exchange.

00:51:30.049 --> 00:51:33.677
I mean I guess that was my message.

00:51:33.677 --> 00:51:36.980
It was like I love you and respect you

00:51:36.980 --> 00:51:39.360
but I don't have time to
invest in a friendship

00:51:39.360 --> 00:51:41.160
in the way that I think you deserve,

00:51:42.000 --> 00:51:45.550
therefore, I just needed
to get this off my chest

00:51:45.550 --> 00:51:46.863
and break up with you?

00:51:47.750 --> 00:51:51.030
Like, I regret it.

00:51:51.030 --> 00:51:55.031
I think about when am I gonna
cross paths with this guy

00:51:55.031 --> 00:51:59.920
in a Ralphs grocery store,
on the street or something

00:51:59.920 --> 00:52:04.020
and like I often think
about what I'm going to say.

00:52:04.020 --> 00:52:06.970
And now, I'm tortured by
that, you know, it's like--

00:52:06.970 --> 00:52:09.910
Well, so let's get back to
her question though, right?

00:52:09.910 --> 00:52:10.743
Yeah.

00:52:10.743 --> 00:52:12.660
Because I think maybe we have

00:52:12.660 --> 00:52:15.010
different perspectives
on this and that's fine.

00:52:16.310 --> 00:52:19.500
And I'm not just saying this is my advice

00:52:19.500 --> 00:52:22.483
because this is what I do because I don't,

00:52:23.910 --> 00:52:26.740
it isn't that I don't feel
guilty about it at all

00:52:26.740 --> 00:52:28.960
like, when we talk about it in this way

00:52:28.960 --> 00:52:30.287
but I think I've made a decision

00:52:30.287 --> 00:52:33.190
and maybe this is a decision
out of self-preservation

00:52:34.880 --> 00:52:36.520
but I mean one of the
things that I have learned

00:52:36.520 --> 00:52:37.950
in therapy is that

00:52:39.590 --> 00:52:43.950
it's okay to make decisions

00:52:43.950 --> 00:52:46.410
that have your own health,

00:52:46.410 --> 00:52:48.770
your own mental well-being in mind,

00:52:48.770 --> 00:52:52.323
and I think that, you know,

00:52:53.670 --> 00:52:55.240
we come from a place

00:52:57.510 --> 00:52:59.520
where I think that there is

00:53:01.170 --> 00:53:03.760
a sense that

00:53:05.120 --> 00:53:07.370
self-sacrifice is super important

00:53:07.370 --> 00:53:09.430
and kinda always putting
other people's needs

00:53:09.430 --> 00:53:10.870
before yours is super important

00:53:10.870 --> 00:53:14.170
and I, in principle,
agree with those things,

00:53:14.170 --> 00:53:15.603
in principle, right?

00:53:16.950 --> 00:53:19.830
But there's sometimes, just because,

00:53:19.830 --> 00:53:22.820
like sometimes, your needs are actually,

00:53:22.820 --> 00:53:24.890
you got to think about what your needs are

00:53:24.890 --> 00:53:27.490
and you have to think about
the reality of a situation.

00:53:27.490 --> 00:53:30.780
And so I think the way that I interact

00:53:30.780 --> 00:53:34.930
with these long-term
friendships is this like,

00:53:34.930 --> 00:53:37.210
okay, when I have the opportunity,

00:53:37.210 --> 00:53:40.320
like a real opportunity
whether that's a text exchange,

00:53:40.320 --> 00:53:42.450
even if it's just initiated
by the other person

00:53:42.450 --> 00:53:44.660
or it's seeing them in person,

00:53:44.660 --> 00:53:47.180
I wanna be present and
I wanna be available

00:53:47.180 --> 00:53:52.180
and I want to be invested in
the friendship for that time

00:53:52.940 --> 00:53:56.430
but then, when we're
not physically together

00:53:56.430 --> 00:53:58.440
and that we kinda go back to our own lives

00:53:58.440 --> 00:54:00.760
because we're not in a situation

00:54:00.760 --> 00:54:01.840
that we're gonna be brought

00:54:01.840 --> 00:54:03.980
into each other's presence
on a regular basis,

00:54:03.980 --> 00:54:06.920
I'm going to be okay with the fact

00:54:06.920 --> 00:54:11.320
that there's not much of
a friendship in the gaps.

00:54:11.320 --> 00:54:12.720
And I'm gonna be okay with that

00:54:12.720 --> 00:54:15.130
because I feel like
trying to make anything

00:54:15.130 --> 00:54:16.993
beyond that happen,
it's gonna be something

00:54:16.993 --> 00:54:19.560
that I'm not going to be good at it

00:54:19.560 --> 00:54:21.530
and it's not gonna be a regular thing

00:54:21.530 --> 00:54:23.490
and it's only going to
lead to disappointment.

00:54:23.490 --> 00:54:24.900
If we can have an understanding

00:54:24.900 --> 00:54:26.710
that there's not gonna be much here

00:54:26.710 --> 00:54:28.900
until we see each other again,

00:54:28.900 --> 00:54:30.630
and hey, let's be open to that.

00:54:30.630 --> 00:54:31.930
Let's try to make that happen,

00:54:31.930 --> 00:54:34.400
if we're gonna be in the
area, I'm gonna let you know

00:54:34.400 --> 00:54:35.350
so we can get together.

00:54:35.350 --> 00:54:37.170
Like, let's be proactive about planning.

00:54:37.170 --> 00:54:39.200
Hey, let's go to see a game
back in Raleigh together

00:54:39.200 --> 00:54:41.350
or something like that or hey,
we're coming to your town,

00:54:41.350 --> 00:54:43.350
come out to our show and let's hang out.

00:54:47.540 --> 00:54:50.010
My advice to these three people

00:54:50.010 --> 00:54:52.553
who are asking about different
situations, it's like,

00:54:54.630 --> 00:54:56.670
and I don't know if,

00:54:56.670 --> 00:54:59.531
some of these situations may
not be like a long distance,

00:54:59.531 --> 00:55:01.960
it may just be like I was
friends with this person

00:55:01.960 --> 00:55:03.100
and now, I'm not.

00:55:03.100 --> 00:55:04.830
My tendency is just to be like

00:55:07.160 --> 00:55:12.160
friendships are very difficult
but I don't, I don't know if,

00:55:13.180 --> 00:55:14.900
it depends on the
situation but I don't know

00:55:14.900 --> 00:55:16.460
if it's worth it to be like,

00:55:16.460 --> 00:55:19.890
all right, I'm going to
place the burden on myself

00:55:19.890 --> 00:55:23.020
that if I don't, if this
friendship is not rekindled,

00:55:23.020 --> 00:55:24.620
there's something wrong with me.

00:55:25.691 --> 00:55:27.410
You know what I'm saying?

00:55:27.410 --> 00:55:30.670
Like I don't think, I don't
think you have to force it.

00:55:30.670 --> 00:55:33.220
Yeah, I do think,

00:55:33.220 --> 00:55:36.230
I appreciate what you're
saying, it's helpful

00:55:36.230 --> 00:55:40.393
because I think friendships are,

00:55:42.342 --> 00:55:47.263
it's a reciprocating
cyclical process, you know?

00:55:49.211 --> 00:55:53.780
It's a relationship and
there's give and there's take

00:55:53.780 --> 00:55:55.910
and take and give.

00:55:55.910 --> 00:56:00.910
And it takes both parties being
on enough of the same page

00:56:01.830 --> 00:56:04.900
to continue to move a friendship forward.

00:56:04.900 --> 00:56:06.240
So if you're wanting to,

00:56:06.240 --> 00:56:08.240
if you're the one wanting
to rekindle the friendship,

00:56:08.240 --> 00:56:09.523
you got to respect the fact

00:56:09.523 --> 00:56:11.580
that that person on the other end of that

00:56:11.580 --> 00:56:14.170
might not be in a position
to make that happen

00:56:14.170 --> 00:56:17.210
or exactly the way that
you would want it to

00:56:17.210 --> 00:56:20.953
or envision it coming together, if at all.

00:56:24.675 --> 00:56:28.210
In the clarifying exchange
that I have with with my friend

00:56:28.210 --> 00:56:30.373
and I do still call him a friend,

00:56:34.550 --> 00:56:38.330
he clarified that he knew
but based on the fact

00:56:38.330 --> 00:56:41.180
that he like, whenever
conversation happen,

00:56:41.180 --> 00:56:43.030
it would be on his initiative.

00:56:43.030 --> 00:56:45.063
So he said, "I actually understand that.

00:56:46.387 --> 00:56:49.407
"So me reaching out was just a kindness."

00:56:50.832 --> 00:56:52.793
There were no strings attached.

00:56:53.720 --> 00:56:54.980
I think that's why I feel bad

00:56:54.980 --> 00:56:59.080
is that like I made
assumptions where there,

00:56:59.080 --> 00:57:03.570
I made wrong assumptions
about why he was saying

00:57:03.570 --> 00:57:05.560
and that like there were strings attached

00:57:05.560 --> 00:57:08.630
that they weren't which
then it was presumptuous

00:57:09.770 --> 00:57:12.010
where he was just being nice, you know?

00:57:12.010 --> 00:57:13.037
Yeah.

00:57:13.037 --> 00:57:14.813
But I think,

00:57:14.813 --> 00:57:18.140
I mean relationships are dicey

00:57:18.140 --> 00:57:21.114
and so you've got to be,

00:57:21.114 --> 00:57:23.800
and you can't, like you
said, you can't force,

00:57:23.800 --> 00:57:27.304
you can't force something to
work if it's not gonna work

00:57:27.304 --> 00:57:30.450
and that doesn't mean you
shouldn't try but you should,

00:57:30.450 --> 00:57:32.120
I think your advice is

00:57:33.950 --> 00:57:38.950
initiate lightly and just
see what comes of it.

00:57:39.250 --> 00:57:41.490
I mean it's just like in
a romantic relationship

00:57:41.490 --> 00:57:42.770
if you're really into somebody,

00:57:42.770 --> 00:57:46.550
you can't lead a horse
to water, so to speak.

00:57:46.550 --> 00:57:48.330
You can't make somebody
fall in love with you.

00:57:48.330 --> 00:57:49.788
But don't date a horse.

00:57:49.788 --> 00:57:50.621
Especially if they're a horse.

00:57:50.621 --> 00:57:51.454
Don't date a horse.

00:57:52.530 --> 00:57:54.903
I mean it's much more,

00:57:56.530 --> 00:57:58.920
we don't, I think we fall back on thinking

00:57:58.920 --> 00:57:59.927
of friendships as being this thing

00:57:59.927 --> 00:58:02.550
that's like, oh, it's so
easy and it just happens

00:58:02.550 --> 00:58:05.610
and it's like, aren't I likable?

00:58:05.610 --> 00:58:06.450
Aren't you likable?

00:58:06.450 --> 00:58:07.790
Well, then, can't we be friends?

00:58:07.790 --> 00:58:09.880
It's like, there's a
lot of freaking factors.

00:58:09.880 --> 00:58:14.300
It's very, I mean in many
ways, it's just as challenging

00:58:14.300 --> 00:58:16.203
as a romantic relationship.

00:58:17.320 --> 00:58:19.560
I think that we may be thinking about this

00:58:19.560 --> 00:58:21.930
in a very narrow way because of our age.

00:58:21.930 --> 00:58:22.763
Mm-hmm.

00:58:23.650 --> 00:58:25.070
Because we just assumed,

00:58:25.070 --> 00:58:26.430
again, we've already established

00:58:26.430 --> 00:58:30.240
that we're not great at
connecting with people digitally.

00:58:30.240 --> 00:58:31.090
Mm-hmm, yeah.

00:58:31.090 --> 00:58:34.580
Via phone or text or heck,
I've got a VR headset now.

00:58:34.580 --> 00:58:37.403
I could sit down in a room
with someone who's got one.

00:58:38.430 --> 00:58:41.570
And maybe physical proximity

00:58:42.740 --> 00:58:46.260
will continue to become
less and less a factor

00:58:46.260 --> 00:58:47.610
in maintaining a friendship

00:58:48.480 --> 00:58:51.070
and maybe that has already happened

00:58:51.070 --> 00:58:54.010
and because I think what
I just said a second ago

00:58:54.010 --> 00:58:58.290
is that hey, listen, if
we're together physically,

00:58:58.290 --> 00:59:01.050
I wanna make an effort to be there for you

00:59:01.050 --> 00:59:05.140
and to be a listening ear and
to catch up and to be present

00:59:05.140 --> 00:59:07.410
but if we're not physically
present together,

00:59:07.410 --> 00:59:09.540
the best I'm going to do is just try

00:59:09.540 --> 00:59:11.880
to be proactive about opportunities for us

00:59:11.880 --> 00:59:15.360
to be physically present
together that are practical.

00:59:15.360 --> 00:59:17.840
And maybe that's a really
narrow way to look at it.

00:59:17.840 --> 00:59:19.370
I understand that about myself

00:59:19.370 --> 00:59:21.140
and you might be like hey, guys, listen,

00:59:21.140 --> 00:59:24.310
my best friends are people
that are in different countries

00:59:24.310 --> 00:59:26.750
that I connect with
that I video chat with.

00:59:26.750 --> 00:59:27.660
what is this?

00:59:27.660 --> 00:59:28.493
Right.

00:59:28.493 --> 00:59:30.230
Most of the people that
we're talking about

00:59:30.230 --> 00:59:34.480
are kinda like us, of our generation

00:59:34.480 --> 00:59:37.860
and we're not video chatting
with a lot of people

00:59:37.860 --> 00:59:40.350
in the way that like our kids will just,

00:59:40.350 --> 00:59:41.510
you were talking about Lincoln

00:59:41.510 --> 00:59:43.930
just being on like a constant video chat

00:59:43.930 --> 00:59:45.220
with a group of friends.

00:59:45.220 --> 00:59:46.053
Yeah.

00:59:47.570 --> 00:59:50.000
On that like Houseparty
app or whatever it was.

00:59:50.000 --> 00:59:50.833
Yeah.

00:59:50.833 --> 00:59:52.440
And I understand, okay, this generation,

00:59:52.440 --> 00:59:54.050
this next generation is different

00:59:54.050 --> 00:59:57.853
so maybe our advice is not
very applicable to them.

00:59:58.740 --> 01:00:00.720
So I don't know where you're
at with these situations.

01:00:00.720 --> 01:00:04.870
I would just say that if you
feel like you need that person,

01:00:04.870 --> 01:00:06.230
like you need that friendship

01:00:06.230 --> 01:00:08.023
or they might need that friendship,

01:00:10.393 --> 01:00:11.970
then I think, like you said,

01:00:11.970 --> 01:00:16.670
sort of initiating but not
initiating where, don't,

01:00:16.670 --> 01:00:20.290
I just, the idea of putting
a burden on yourself

01:00:20.290 --> 01:00:21.830
and feeling this,

01:00:21.830 --> 01:00:25.730
all of a sudden there's a
negative obligation or guilt

01:00:25.730 --> 01:00:28.340
that's associated with your
interaction with that person,

01:00:28.340 --> 01:00:32.184
to me, I just don't like that negativity.

01:00:32.184 --> 01:00:35.970
When I think about somebody
we went to college with,

01:00:35.970 --> 01:00:38.390
I've got good memories and
I think about the next time

01:00:38.390 --> 01:00:40.840
we're hanging out, we're
gonna have a good time

01:00:40.840 --> 01:00:41.673
but I don't wanna think about

01:00:41.673 --> 01:00:43.990
and then every other
time I think about them,

01:00:43.990 --> 01:00:46.140
all I do is just associate guilt

01:00:46.140 --> 01:00:48.150
with the fact that I
don't talk to them enough.

01:00:48.150 --> 01:00:48.983
Yeah, it's not--

01:00:48.983 --> 01:00:49.970
It doesn't seem healthy.

01:00:49.970 --> 01:00:51.190
It's not.

01:00:51.190 --> 01:00:54.550
So how can I focus on
what the positive is?

01:00:54.550 --> 01:00:56.240
But like you said, if they're
going through something,

01:00:56.240 --> 01:00:57.870
they need somebody to talk to,

01:00:57.870 --> 01:01:00.270
they need somebody to show up for them

01:01:00.270 --> 01:01:01.860
whether that's physically or digitally,

01:01:01.860 --> 01:01:04.003
then I hope that I can do that,

01:01:05.666 --> 01:01:07.540
and I feel an obligation to do that.

01:01:07.540 --> 01:01:09.430
But that doesn't mean, in every sense,

01:01:09.430 --> 01:01:12.080
it's appropriate or the
right choice for you

01:01:12.080 --> 01:01:13.260
to even do that.

01:01:13.260 --> 01:01:14.093
Right.

01:01:14.093 --> 01:01:15.060
I think we do need to say that.

01:01:15.060 --> 01:01:19.120
It's like just because
somebody needs a friend,

01:01:19.120 --> 01:01:20.980
there has to be scenarios

01:01:20.980 --> 01:01:23.430
where that doesn't automatically mean

01:01:23.430 --> 01:01:24.790
you have to be that friend.

01:01:24.790 --> 01:01:25.623
Right.

01:01:25.623 --> 01:01:28.780
So, I'm just trying to tip the scales

01:01:28.780 --> 01:01:30.910
and I'm saying that more for myself.

01:01:30.910 --> 01:01:34.380
And again, that wasn't actually the case.

01:01:34.380 --> 01:01:36.470
I think I might have brought
some of that assumption

01:01:36.470 --> 01:01:39.963
but that wasn't the case with
this scenario that I explored.

01:01:43.320 --> 01:01:46.983
But I actually don't
know where you, is it?

01:01:49.200 --> 01:01:52.370
Where do you come down
on this text exchange?

01:01:52.370 --> 01:01:55.850
That, I mean if you're saying
you would have just been nice

01:01:55.850 --> 01:01:58.080
and like you would have
continued the niceties

01:01:58.080 --> 01:02:00.480
and then I mean, and then the opposite,

01:02:00.480 --> 01:02:02.560
that's one extreme, right?

01:02:02.560 --> 01:02:03.393
Is it not?

01:02:03.393 --> 01:02:05.450
And the other extreme
is clearly what I did,

01:02:05.450 --> 01:02:08.040
which is basically
break off the friendship

01:02:08.040 --> 01:02:09.290
because I couldn't be

01:02:12.110 --> 01:02:16.280
everything I thought I had to
be which wasn't even the case.

01:02:16.280 --> 01:02:17.113
I don't know.

01:02:17.113 --> 01:02:19.663
I mean, I definitely am, like I said,

01:02:20.850 --> 01:02:24.560
my tendency is to maintain
the status quo and I don't,

01:02:24.560 --> 01:02:26.560
I really don't like
uncomfortable situations.

01:02:26.560 --> 01:02:29.310
I don't like awkwardness,
I don't like conflict

01:02:29.310 --> 01:02:30.770
and so it's really difficult for me

01:02:30.770 --> 01:02:31.940
when I'm not like, okay,

01:02:31.940 --> 01:02:34.530
I need to be honest with this person

01:02:34.530 --> 01:02:37.850
so that we can clarify where we're at

01:02:37.850 --> 01:02:40.080
so we can actually reach
some sort of conclusion.

01:02:40.080 --> 01:02:41.340
Like I'm much more likely to be like,

01:02:41.340 --> 01:02:43.240
okay, this is going to
be this sort of thing

01:02:43.240 --> 01:02:46.170
that just indefinitely continues

01:02:46.170 --> 01:02:48.470
and isn't really that
big of an inconvenience.

01:02:48.470 --> 01:02:50.340
It's easy to just send back a text

01:02:50.340 --> 01:02:52.360
that says, miss you too, man.

01:02:52.360 --> 01:02:55.500
But when it's like, hey,
let's get together and do this

01:02:55.500 --> 01:02:58.940
and it's like, I've got these people

01:02:58.940 --> 01:03:02.220
that I feel like I need to prioritize

01:03:02.220 --> 01:03:05.330
because they're like
more in my inner circle

01:03:05.330 --> 01:03:09.010
and I can't say yes to them right now

01:03:09.010 --> 01:03:12.170
and give them what I want to give them.

01:03:12.170 --> 01:03:15.223
Forget what they need, what I
wanna give them as a friend,

01:03:16.690 --> 01:03:19.240
then at that point, you got to,

01:03:19.240 --> 01:03:20.120
you're just lying and saying,

01:03:20.120 --> 01:03:22.280
actually I can't get
together, I have plans.

01:03:22.280 --> 01:03:24.340
It's like you're just letting
people read between the lines.

01:03:24.340 --> 01:03:27.873
It's like okay, you can
see that you're initiating.

01:03:30.155 --> 01:03:32.543
I'm not a bad person for just,

01:03:34.190 --> 01:03:36.370
I think most people can get the message.

01:03:36.370 --> 01:03:38.243
It's like hey, it's not
that I don't like you,

01:03:38.243 --> 01:03:41.500
it's just that I don't have capacity.

01:03:41.500 --> 01:03:43.010
Yeah, I think my theory

01:03:43.010 --> 01:03:46.160
is that you arrive at the same result

01:03:46.160 --> 01:03:49.250
with a little less awkwardness.

01:03:49.250 --> 01:03:51.070
And it might take a little
bit longer to get there

01:03:51.070 --> 01:03:52.380
for somebody to get the message.

01:03:52.380 --> 01:03:54.180
Like, oh, he's not really available.

01:03:55.316 --> 01:03:59.280
I don't necessarily think
that one is better or worse.

01:03:59.280 --> 01:04:00.900
You were ripping the Band-Aid off,

01:04:00.900 --> 01:04:03.470
I'm letting the Band-Aid
fall off in the shower.

01:04:03.470 --> 01:04:04.950
You know what I'm saying?

01:04:04.950 --> 01:04:08.262
And maybe it should
have come out a little--

01:04:08.262 --> 01:04:09.190
I wouldn't shower with someone

01:04:09.190 --> 01:04:11.226
who's not that close of
a friend but go ahead.

01:04:11.226 --> 01:04:14.190
Maybe the Band-Aid should have come off

01:04:14.190 --> 01:04:15.190
a little bit earlier

01:04:17.430 --> 01:04:18.490
but I tend to,

01:04:18.490 --> 01:04:21.750
it really takes a lot for me

01:04:21.750 --> 01:04:24.540
to have an uncomfortable
conversation with somebody, so--

01:04:24.540 --> 01:04:25.373
Well, I--

01:04:25.373 --> 01:04:26.206
I tend to--

01:04:26.206 --> 01:04:28.030
I need to take this to therapy, clearly.

01:04:28.030 --> 01:04:28.980
Yes, you do.

01:04:28.980 --> 01:04:31.027
I mean I think there's
probably some deep-rooted

01:04:31.027 --> 01:04:32.970
fear of abandonment thing

01:04:32.970 --> 01:04:34.510
that then I don't wanna
put on other people

01:04:34.510 --> 01:04:37.070
even when they're not even expecting

01:04:37.070 --> 01:04:38.420
or fearing that themselves.

01:04:38.420 --> 01:04:39.770
I would say that that would
be something to look for.

01:04:39.770 --> 01:04:41.670
I think it's probably
something in that arena.

01:04:41.670 --> 01:04:45.411
So, I mean we can leave it at that.

01:04:45.411 --> 01:04:46.244
Okay.

01:04:46.244 --> 01:04:49.150
It seems I'm pressing
you to solve my problem.

01:04:49.150 --> 01:04:50.190
I don't have a solution.

01:04:50.190 --> 01:04:51.660
I'm just telling you the
way that I would do it

01:04:51.660 --> 01:04:54.370
and it tends to be okay.

01:04:54.370 --> 01:04:55.203
Yeah.

01:04:56.840 --> 01:05:00.380
I've definitely slowed my role
on saying what I'm thinking,

01:05:00.380 --> 01:05:02.370
which brings it back to this still

01:05:02.370 --> 01:05:05.713
may be obnoxious at times
level that you're used to.

01:05:06.920 --> 01:05:08.280
I don't know how much help we've been.

01:05:08.280 --> 01:05:09.530
We didn't answer a lot of questions.

01:05:09.530 --> 01:05:11.076
We did answer three questions at once.

01:05:11.076 --> 01:05:11.909
You know what?

01:05:11.909 --> 01:05:12.742
It was--
And we took a lot,

01:05:12.742 --> 01:05:13.575
I mean it was more--

01:05:13.575 --> 01:05:14.610
Hey, it was helpful for me, thank you.

01:05:14.610 --> 01:05:17.080
We did what we often do which
is we take your questions

01:05:17.080 --> 01:05:18.728
and then we make it--

01:05:18.728 --> 01:05:19.561
So what?

01:05:19.561 --> 01:05:21.430
So you're now, you're
apologizing for that?

01:05:21.430 --> 01:05:25.630
An internal exploration of
our own lives but hopefully,

01:05:25.630 --> 01:05:28.450
we're all learning together
as a large community

01:05:28.450 --> 01:05:31.653
and hopefully, we're getting
better at doing things,

01:05:32.490 --> 01:05:34.163
friendships, as we go.

01:05:35.150 --> 01:05:37.200
I quite enjoyed the conversation

01:05:37.200 --> 01:05:40.890
and I'm kind of nervous about
it going out there but--

01:05:40.890 --> 01:05:42.170
Why?
Whatever.

01:05:42.170 --> 01:05:43.670
You got nothing to apologize about

01:05:43.670 --> 01:05:44.870
and if you do, you will.

01:05:47.520 --> 01:05:49.390
I do have a Rec in Effect and--

01:05:49.390 --> 01:05:50.223
Okay.

01:05:51.240 --> 01:05:54.150
Mine is a book that I am listening to

01:05:54.150 --> 01:05:57.610
that everybody's talking about this book.

01:05:57.610 --> 01:06:00.590
Oh, you gotta read it or listen to it.

01:06:00.590 --> 01:06:03.570
I'm half way through it, it's
captivating us enthralling.

01:06:03.570 --> 01:06:05.923
It's "Catch and Kill" by Ronan Farrow.

01:06:07.580 --> 01:06:10.140
So, of course, Ronan
Farrow, he is most known

01:06:10.140 --> 01:06:13.840
as being Woody Allen and Mia Farrow's son

01:06:15.020 --> 01:06:18.420
even though he, for
understandable reasons,

01:06:18.420 --> 01:06:20.810
likes to distance himself
from his famous parents

01:06:20.810 --> 01:06:24.800
and he has a career of
his own as a journalist

01:06:24.800 --> 01:06:26.017
and he was the--

01:06:26.017 --> 01:06:27.390
Lot of help you just gave him.

01:06:27.390 --> 01:06:30.490
He was the one who was very instrumental

01:06:30.490 --> 01:06:33.830
in sort of breaking the
Harvey Weinstein's story.

01:06:33.830 --> 01:06:34.663
Ah.

01:06:34.663 --> 01:06:37.150
And it's just this incredibly

01:06:37.150 --> 01:06:40.870
engrossing tale of him

01:06:40.870 --> 01:06:44.000
trying to get to the bottom of the story

01:06:44.000 --> 01:06:45.890
and just be able to report it

01:06:45.890 --> 01:06:48.700
and all the crazy stuff
that he encountered

01:06:48.700 --> 01:06:50.980
in the process of going after someone

01:06:50.980 --> 01:06:54.070
as big and as powerful and
as with as many connections

01:06:54.070 --> 01:06:55.113
as Harvey Weinstein.

01:06:56.210 --> 01:06:58.550
Just fascinating story.

01:06:58.550 --> 01:07:01.670
So it's a docu-novel.

01:07:01.670 --> 01:07:05.870
Yeah, and his, the audiobook,
which he was actually,

01:07:05.870 --> 01:07:07.210
some people made fun of him for this

01:07:07.210 --> 01:07:08.260
but I thought it was great.

01:07:08.260 --> 01:07:09.930
Oh, okay.

01:07:09.930 --> 01:07:14.040
He does all the voices for his audiobook

01:07:14.040 --> 01:07:17.430
and he like does like
impersonations of the people

01:07:17.430 --> 01:07:19.730
that he spoke with whether
they be Australian,

01:07:19.730 --> 01:07:24.000
British or a woman with a sultry voice

01:07:24.000 --> 01:07:26.100
and he basically just does all the accents

01:07:26.100 --> 01:07:28.508
an he does a really
good job at it, I think.

01:07:28.508 --> 01:07:30.440
But it's nonfiction.

01:07:30.440 --> 01:07:31.273
It's not a fiction.

01:07:31.273 --> 01:07:33.410
This is all any place,
like there's a recording,

01:07:33.410 --> 01:07:36.030
there's the famous recording
of Harvey Weinstein

01:07:36.030 --> 01:07:40.990
that one of the actresses
that he was harassing took.

01:07:43.090 --> 01:07:43.923
Wow.

01:07:43.923 --> 01:07:45.070
And it's in there.

01:07:45.070 --> 01:07:48.767
Anyway, fascinating story if
you're into that kind of thing.

01:07:48.767 --> 01:07:50.130
"Catch and Kill".

01:07:50.130 --> 01:07:51.890
I'm recommending the audiobook

01:07:51.890 --> 01:07:53.207
